    # Anxiety & Grounding
    "Box breathing exercise: Inhale for 4 counts, hold for 4 counts, exhale for 4 counts, hold for 4 counts. Repeat 4 times. This activates your parasympathetic nervous system and reduces anxiety.",
    "5-4-3-2-1 grounding technique: Name 5 things you can see, 4 things you can touch, 3 things you can hear, 2 things you can smell, 1 thing you can taste. This brings you back to the present moment.",
    "Progressive muscle relaxation: Starting from your toes, tense each muscle group for 5 seconds then release. Work your way up to your head. This releases physical tension caused by anxiety.",
    "When feeling anxious, try the 4-7-8 breathing technique: inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, exhale slowly for 8 seconds. Do this 3 to 4 times.",
    "Grounding yourself physically can help during anxiety: press your feet firmly into the floor, hold a cold glass of water, or splash cold water on your face to reset your nervous system.",

    # Sadness & Low Mood
    "Behavioral activation for low mood: When feeling sad, do one small enjoyable activity even if you don't feel like it. Start with just 5 minutes. Action comes before motivation, not after.",
    "When feeling sad, try writing down 3 small things you are grateful for today. They don't have to be big. This gently shifts focus without dismissing your pain.",
    "Opposite action technique for sadness: If sadness tells you to isolate, gently do the opposite — send one text to a friend, step outside for 5 minutes, or sit near a window.",
    "Self-compassion practice: Place your hand on your heart and say to yourself — this is a moment of suffering, suffering is part of life, may I be kind to myself right now.",
    "Low mood often distorts thinking. Ask yourself: would I say this thought to a close friend who was struggling? If not, try reframing it with the same kindness you would offer them.",

    # Stress & Overwhelm
    "When overwhelmed, use the brain dump technique: write every single worry or task on paper without filtering. Then circle only the one thing you can act on today. The rest can wait.",
    "If you are feeling overwhelmed by tasks, try the 2 minute rule: if something takes less than 2 minutes to do, do it now. This clears mental clutter quickly.",
    "Body scan meditation for stress: Close your eyes, take 3 deep breaths, then slowly scan from head to toe noticing where you hold tension. Simply noticing without judgment reduces stress.",
    "When stressed, STOP technique: Stop what you are doing, Take a breath, Observe your thoughts and feelings without judgment, Proceed with awareness.",
    "Stress often comes from trying to control things outside our control. Write two lists: things I can control and things I cannot. Focus your energy only on the first list.",

    # Negative Thoughts & CBT Core
    "Cognitive restructuring: When a negative thought appears, ask yourself these 3 questions — Is this thought based on facts or feelings? What is the evidence for and against it? What would I tell a friend who had this thought?",
    "Thought challenging technique: Identify the automatic negative thought, rate how much you believe it from 0 to 100, write evidence for and against it, then create a balanced alternative thought and re-rate your belief.",
    "Common cognitive distortions include all-or-nothing thinking, catastrophizing, mind reading, and overgeneralization. Simply naming the distortion when you notice it reduces its power over you.",
    "Journaling prompt for negative thoughts: Write the situation, your automatic thought, the emotion it caused, evidence for the thought, evidence against the thought, and a more balanced perspective.",

